---

<BaseLayout
  title="Rho — Open Vision-Language-Action Models for Robotics"
  description="Rho is a family of open-weight vision-language-action models for bimanual robotic manipulation and efficient embodiment adaptation."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container container--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">Open-weight vision-language-action models</p>
      <h1>Rho</h1>
      <p class="hero__dek">
        A family of general-purpose physical AI models for bimanual manipulation,
        combining a physically grounded Phi-family backbone with a flow-matching
        action expert and a progressive embodiment-adaptation recipe.
      </p>
      <div class="hero__links">
        {resources.map((resource) => (
          <a class="button" href={resource.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            {resource.label}<span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        ))}
        <span class="button button--soon" role="link" aria-disabled="true" title="arXiv paper coming soon">
          Paper (arXiv) — coming soon
        </span>
      </div>
      <dl class="hero__stats">
        <div><dt>LIBERO average</dt><dd>97.9%</dd></div>
        <div><dt>RoboEval average</dt><dd>73.4%</dd></div>
        <div><dt>FR3 Duo physical success</dt><dd>80.0%</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="hero__stats-note">
        FR3 Duo task success vs π0.5 46.7% · GR00T&nbsp;N1.7 50.0% — 30 rollouts per task, per model.
      </p>
      <div class="hero__media hero__media--sizzle" aria-label="Rho manipulation examples">
        <figure>
          <img
            src={withBase("assets/fr3-duo-sizzle.webp")}
            alt="FR3 Duo highlight reel"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>FR3 Duo · precision bimanual manipulation</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <img
            src={withBase("assets/ur5e-sizzle.webp")}
            alt="UR5e AI Trainer highlight reel"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>UR5e AI Trainer · pick-and-place workflows</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <img
            src={withBase("assets/yambox-sizzle.webp")}
            alt="YAMBox highlight reel"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>YAMBox · bimanual panel and control-board tasks</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <nav class="section-nav" aria-label="Page sections">
    <div class="container container--wide">
      <a href="#overview">Overview</a>
      <a href="#adaptation">Training recipe</a>
      <a href="#method">Method</a>
      <a href="#embodiments">Embodiments</a>
      <a href="#benchmarks">Benchmarks</a>
      <a href="#efficiency">Efficiency</a>
      <a href="#models">Models</a>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <div class="page-content">
    <section id="overview" class="section container">
      <p class="section-kicker">Overview</p>
      <h2>Broad perception, precise control, efficient adaptation</h2>
      <div class="lede-grid">
        <p class="lede">
          Rho is an open-weight vision-language-action (VLA) model designed for
          data-efficient adaptation to new robots and new tasks. It couples a
          physically grounded backbone from Microsoft's Phi model family to a
          flow-matching action expert that emits continuous action chunks, trained
          with a recipe we call <strong>regularized progressive adaptation</strong>:
          each stage narrows broad visual-language understanding toward deployable
          control, while vision-language co-training runs alongside the first three
          stages so the backbone's perception is preserved.
        </p>
        <p>
          Crucially, Rho separates adapting to a <em>robot</em> from adapting to a
          <em>task</em>. One pretrained base is specialized to a platform through
          horizontal mid-training, yielding reusable embodiment checkpoints that
          make each downstream task cheaper to learn. Pretrained for a
          <strong>single epoch</strong> over a 3,219.5-hour, 337.6-million-frame
          robot corpus, Rho matches or outperforms larger open VLAs across LIBERO
          (97.9%), RoboEval (73.37%), RoboTwin, and physical manipulation. The base
          model is available as
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/microsoft/rho-base">microsoft/rho-base</a>,
          along with mid-trained checkpoints for
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/microsoft/rho-fr3-duo">FR3 Duo</a>,
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/microsoft/rho-ur-ai-trainer">UR AI Trainer</a>,
          and <a href="https://huggingface.co/microsoft/rho-yam-box">YAMBox</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article>
          <span class="feature-number">01</span>
          <h3>Physically grounded VLM</h3>
          <p>Additional robotics-relevant grounding, multi-image reasoning, OCR, counting, and synthetic visual reasoning data.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="feature-number">02</span>
          <h3>Flow-matching action expert</h3>
          <p>A compact continuous-action decoder with cross-attention to VLM context and configurable action chunks.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="feature-number">03</span>
          <h3>Flexible LeRobot training</h3>
          <p>YAML-configured datasets, transforms, key mappings, action representations, and multi-dataset training.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="adaptation" class="section adaptation-section">
      <div class="container container--wide">
        <p class="section-kicker">Training recipe</p>
        <h2>Regularized progressive adaptation</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Rho is trained through four stages that progressively narrow broad
          visual-language understanding into embodiment knowledge and, finally, a
          deployable task policy. Vision-language co-training regularizes the first
          three stages so the backbone's perception is preserved; only the final
          task fine-tuning is robot-only. Each stage trains on increasingly
          specific data — general physical grounding, then multi-embodiment robot
          data, then a single embodiment, and finally a single deployment task —
          while the compute recipe stays consistent across embodiments so the same
          batch size and learning-rate schedule ports from FR3 Duo to UR AI
          Trainer to YAMBox.
        </p>

        <div class="adaptation-flow" aria-label="Four-stage Rho training pipeline">
          {adaptationStages.map((stage, index) => (
            <article class="adaptation-stage">
              <div class="adaptation-stage__topline">
                <span class="adaptation-stage__number">{stage.number}</span>
                <span class="adaptation-stage__label">{stage.label}</span>
              </div>
              <h3>{stage.title}</h3>
              <p class="adaptation-stage__subtitle">{stage.subtitle}</p>
              <p class="adaptation-stage__description">{stage.description}</p>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>Data</dt><dd>{stage.data}</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Mixture</dt><dd>{stage.mix}</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Output</dt><dd>{stage.output}</dd></div>
              </dl>
              {index < adaptationStages.length - 1 && (
                <span class="adaptation-stage__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              )}
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div class="adaptation-extension">
          <div class="adaptation-extension__icon" aria-hidden="true">↻</div>
          <div>
            <strong>Optional online extension</strong>
            <p>
              After offline fine-tuning, a lightweight policy can adapt the action
              generator's initial latent noise from corrective demonstrations or
              task rewards while keeping the flow-matching generator fixed.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="adaptation-details">
          <article>
            <span>Phi-Phy</span>
            <h3>Ground physical understanding first</h3>
            <p>
              The 4.68B-parameter Phi-family backbone uses SigLIP 2 NaFlex vision
              features and is trained on robotics-relevant grounding, pointing,
              spatial reasoning, OCR, counting, and synthetic multi-image tasks.
              It does not yet predict actions.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span>Pretraining</span>
            <h3>Learn a shared action representation</h3>
            <p>
              Robot data from OXE, XDOF, Scale, and OmniReset trains the complete
              VLA, while RoboPoint and RefSpatial batches continue to regularize the
              backbone's visual-language capabilities.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span>Midtraining</span>
            <h3>Learn the robot, not one task</h3>
            <p>
              Broad multitask data from a single embodiment teaches camera layout,
              arm geometry, gripper conventions, temporal dynamics, and workspace
              characteristics that transfer across downstream tasks.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span>Fine-tuning</span>
            <h3>Specialize for deployment</h3>
            <p>
              Target-task demonstrations refine the pretrained or midtrained model
              into a policy for a specific task set. Starting from an embodiment
              checkpoint is especially valuable in low-data settings.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>

        <details class="checkpoint-details">
          <summary>Released checkpoints and training step counts</summary>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Checkpoint</th><th>Stage</th><th>Global batch</th><th>Steps</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>rho-base</code> (Hero2 · epoch 1)</th>
                  <td>Pretrain</td>
                  <td>3,072</td>
                  <td>212,000 (1 epoch)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>rho-ur-ai-trainer</code></th>
                  <td>Embodiment midtrain</td>
                  <td>1,024</td>
                  <td>130,000 (2 epochs)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>rho-yam-box</code></th>
                  <td>Embodiment midtrain</td>
                  <td>1,024</td>
                  <td>90,000 (2 epochs)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>rho-fr3-duo</code></th>
                  <td>Embodiment midtrain</td>
                  <td>1,024</td>
                  <td>68,000 (2 epochs)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>rho-robotwin-midtrain</code></th>
                  <td>Controlled midtrain</td>
                  <td>1,024</td>
                  <td>2 epochs</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">RoboEval evaluation policy</th>
                  <td>Task fine-tune</td>
                  <td>128</td>
                  <td>40,000</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">LIBERO evaluation policy</th>
                  <td>Task fine-tune</td>
                  <td>128</td>
                  <td>40,000</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>rho-libero</code> noise-head</th>
                  <td>FlowDAgger refinement</td>
                  <td>—</td>
                  <td>3,000</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="method" class="section section--tint">
      <div class="container container--wide">
        <p class="section-kicker">Architecture</p>
        <h2>Rho flow-matching action expert</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Visual observations, language instructions, and robot state condition a
          continuous action expert that turns Gaussian noise into a chunk of future
          actions by flow matching: at inference the learned velocity field is
          integrated from noise to actions in just <strong>10 explicit Euler
          steps</strong>. The expert is a 12-block transformer (width 2,048,
          grouped-query attention with 16 query heads and 4 key/value heads, head
          dimension 128) cross-attending to the Phi-Phy backbone. Rather than
          learning a separate modulation network per block, Rho shares one set of
          adaLN modulation maps across all blocks and gives each block a small
          zero-initialized offset — the <strong>adaLN-single</strong> design. We
          swept action-expert shapes and sizes on RoboEval and deliberately chose a
          compact one: sharing the modulation weights compresses the expert from
          1.65B to <strong>542M parameters with no measured loss in success
          rate</strong> (0.673 vs 0.671 on RoboEval), keeping Rho fast to train and
          cheap to deploy while preserving the width that matters most for
          contact-rich, precise manipulation.
        </p>
        <figure class="figure figure--paper">
          <a href={withBase("assets/action_expert.pdf")} target="_blank">
            <img src={withBase("assets/action-expert.png")} alt="Rho flow-matching action expert architecture" />
          </a>
          <figcaption>
            State and noisy-action tokens pass through 12 transformer blocks with
            grouped-query self-attention, cross-attention to VLM context, and
            flow-timestep conditioning. Select the figure to open the PDF.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <tbody>
              {modelSpecs.map(([component, specification]) => (
                <tr><th scope="row">{component}</th><td>{specification}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <aside class="callout" id="latent-adaptation" aria-label="Latent-space adaptation compatibility">
          <p class="section-kicker">Deployment adaptation</p>
          <h3>Natively compatible with latent-space adaptation</h3>
          <p>
            Because Rho generates continuous actions with a flow-matching expert, its trajectory
            distribution can be steered or refined in the initial-noise latent space without
            modifying the generator weights. Rho's released checkpoints work with two independent
            recipes:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>FlowDAgger</strong> — interactive imitation learning from corrective
              demonstrations; the FlowDAgger project reports results on Rho.
              <a href="https://microsoft.github.io/FlowDAgger/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Project page ↗</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>DSRL</strong> — diffusion steering for reinforcement-learning-style latent
              adaptation from reward signals.
              <a href="https://diffusion-steering.github.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Project page ↗</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="note">
            Usage and example configurations are in the
            <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/rhobotics" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">rhobotics codebase</a>.
            Empirical adaptation results will follow in a dedicated post.
          </p>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="embodiments" class="section section--dark">
      <div class="container container--wide">
        <p class="section-kicker">Physical systems</p>
        <h2>Embodiment-targeted mid-training</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Mid-training turns the general base model into a reusable, platform-specific
          policy — learning a robot's cameras, kinematics, and control conventions
          once so that downstream tasks start from a strong, embodiment-aware
          checkpoint. Rho is mid-trained on a balanced mixture of robot data and
          robotics-relevant visual reasoning data before downstream fine-tuning.
          Each checkpoint was trained for roughly two epochs at a global batch size
          of 1,024 on eight B200 GPUs. Because our physical tasks span multiple
          steps, we report both
          <strong>success rate</strong> (only fully completed sequences count) and
          <strong>mean task progress</strong> (the fraction of each task's steps
          completed) so partial competence on hard multi-stage tasks is visible
          rather than hidden.
        </p>
        <div class="embodiment-grid">
          {embodiments.map((embodiment) => (
            <article class="embodiment-card">
              <img src={withBase(embodiment.image)} alt={embodiment.name} loading="lazy" />
              <div>
                <span class="badge" title="Two arms × (x, y, z end-effector position + 6D rotation + gripper) = 20 continuous action dimensions">Dual-arm · 20 action dims</span>
                <h3>{embodiment.name}</h3>
                <a href={embodiment.checkpoint}>Checkpoint ↗</a>
              </div>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="table-wrap table-wrap--dark">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Dataset</th><th>Episodes</th><th>Frames</th><th>FPS</th><th>Original hours</th><th>Sampled hours</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {midtrainingRows.map((row) => (
                <tr>{row.map((value, index) => index === 0 ? <th scope="row">{value}</th> : <td>{value}</td>)}</tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="note note--dark">
          YAMBox was subsampled to roughly 300 hours, distributed evenly across task
          types, to align its mid-training volume with the other physical datasets.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section container container--wide">
      <div class="hardware-result">
        <div>
          <p class="section-kicker">FR3 Duo</p>
          <h2>Reliable multi-stage manipulation</h2>
          <p>
            Rho-FR3-Duo is specialized to a dual-arm Franka platform on ~300 hours
            of demonstrations spanning 19 task families of bimanual, contact-rich
            behavior. Across plug insertion, tumbler manipulation, and test-tube
            racking — long, multi-step manipulation sequences — Rho achieves
            <strong>80.0% success and 88.7% mean task progress</strong>, ahead of
            π0.5 (46.7% / 64.5%) and GR00T N1.7 (50.0% / 71.3%).
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            Success rate credits only fully completed task sequences; mean task
            progress credits the fraction of each task's steps that were completed.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href="https://huggingface.co/microsoft/rho-fr3-duo">
            Download rho-fr3-duo ↗
          </a>
        </div>
        <figure class="figure hardware-result__chart">
          <img src={withBase("assets/fig-fr3-duo.png")} alt="FR3 Duo success rate and mean task progress" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Success rate and mean task progress across the five FR3 Duo evaluation tasks.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="task-clips task-clips--fr3 hardware-result__media" aria-label="FR3 Duo task examples">
          <figure>
            <video src={withBase("assets/fr3-beaker-open.mp4")} aria-label="FR3 Duo opening a beaker" controls autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"></video>
            <figcaption>Opening a beaker</figcaption>
          </figure>
