      <article class="result-block">
        <div class="result-block__header">
          <div>
            <h3>
              <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.00435" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                RoboEval
              </a>
            </h3>
            <p>
              RoboEval is a bimanual-manipulation benchmark that scores not just
              task success but <em>how</em> a policy moves — task progression,
              motion efficiency, coordination, and safety. We adapt each model
              under matched benchmark data budgets and evaluate all eight tasks
              across three seeds and 100 rollouts per seed (n = 300 rollouts per
              task). Mean success over three evaluation passes; error bars show
              sample standard deviation. Baseline overall-success numbers in the
              figure below are <strong>our re-runs</strong> under the
              matched-adaptation protocol; the behavioral diagnostics further down
              are the published RoboEval-protocol point estimates.
            </p>
          </div>
          <strong class="metric">73.37% overall</strong>
        </div>
        <figure class="figure figure--wide">
          <img src={withBase("assets/fig-roboeval.png")} alt="RoboEval success rate by task and model" loading="lazy" />
        </figure>
        <div class="diagnostic-grid" aria-label="RoboEval behavioral and safety diagnostics">
          <div class="diagnostic-card">
            <span class="diagnostic-card__val">13× fewer</span>
            <span class="diagnostic-card__lbl">Self-collisions</span>
            <span class="diagnostic-card__sub">0.06 vs 0.79 per episode (MolmoAct2)</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diagnostic-card">
            <span class="diagnostic-card__val">1.77 m</span>
            <span class="diagnostic-card__lbl">Cartesian path length</span>
            <span class="diagnostic-card__sub">vs 1.96 m — more direct motions</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diagnostic-card">
            <span class="diagnostic-card__val">0.07 m</span>
            <span class="diagnostic-card__lbl">Bimanual gripper height Δ</span>
            <span class="diagnostic-card__sub">vs 0.09 m — tighter arm coordination</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="note">
          Behavioral and safety metrics on the combined RoboEval task variations
          (report Table 12). Baseline figures are published RoboEval-protocol point
          estimates.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="result-block">
        <div class="result-block__header">
          <div>
            <h3>RoboTwin</h3>
            <p>
              RoboTwin was built to demonstrate the value of mid-training, which
              makes it the natural simulated benchmark for isolating that effect in
              Rho. We depart from the standard RoboTwin protocol on purpose:
              we choose the dual-arm <strong>UR5e</strong> embodiment (heavily
              represented in our pretraining data), mid-train on 45 of its tasks
              (two epochs at global batch 1,024, with the same VL co-training as
              elsewhere), and hold out five distinct tasks — <em>Hanging Mug, Move
              Stapler Pad, Place Burger Fries, Handover Mic, and Stack Bowls
              Three</em> — for downstream fine-tuning and evaluation. We then
              fine-tune from either the pretrained base or the mid-trained
              checkpoint on progressively smaller slices of downstream data
              (100% → 50% → 25% → 12.5%). At the 12.5% budget, mid-training lifts
              Easy-protocol success from
              <strong>28.1% to 40.6% (+12.5 pts)</strong> and Hard-protocol success
              from <strong>30.9% to 38.9% (+8.0 pts)</strong> — a controlled read
              on how much the embodiment prior helps when task data is scarce.
            </p>
          </div>
          <a class="text-link" href="https://robotwin-platform.github.io/leaderboard">
            RoboTwin leaderboard ↗
          </a>
        </div>
        <figure class="figure figure--wide">
