    <section id="efficiency" class="section section--tint">
      <div class="container container--wide">
        <p class="section-kicker">Systems</p>
        <h2>Training efficiency</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Rho offers low-memory full and expert-only training profiles. Lower values
          are better for peak VRAM and seconds per step.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="results-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Framework</th><th>Recipe</th><th>Trainable</th><th>GC</th><th>Peak VRAM ↓</th><th>Time/step ↓</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {efficiencyRows.map((row) => (
                <tr class:list={{ highlight: row[0] === "Rho" }}>
                  {row.map((value, index) => index < 2 ? <th scope={index === 0 ? "row" : undefined}>{value}</th> : <td>{value}</td>)}
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="note">
          These are observed training profiles rather than a model-quality
          benchmark. Precision, gradient checkpointing, trainable scope, and NFS
          differ across recipes. MolmoAct2 measurements use a batch size of 16;
          its expert-only runs set
          <code>--policy.train_action_expert_only=true</code>.
        </p>

        <div class="efficiency-study">
          <h3>Inference latency and deployment footprint</h3>
          <p>
            Rho predicts a 50-step action chunk per forward solve — the longest horizon
            among the compared models — so each inference emits far more future control
            than peers (π0.5 18, GR00T 40, MolmoAct2 16). The VLM visual context is encoded
            once and reused across all 10 flow-integration steps, sustaining a >10 Hz
            replanning loop that is real-time-chunking compatible.
          </p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Framework</th><th>Flow steps</th><th>Horizon</th><th>Latency (bs=1)</th><th>bs=4</th><th>bs=8</th><th>VRAM (bs=1)</th><th>VRAM (bs=8)</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">OpenPI π0.5</th><td>10</td><td>18</td><td>44.8 ms (22 Hz)</td><td>112 ms</td><td>201 ms</td><td>7.4 GB</td><td>8.0 GB</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">GR00T N1.7</th><td>4</td><td>40</td><td>48.2 ms (21 Hz)</td><td>66 ms</td><td>112 ms</td><td>7.1 GB</td><td>8.4 GB</td></tr>
                <tr class="highlight"><th scope="row">Rho</th><td>10</td><td>50</td><td>83.9 ms (12 Hz)</td><td>126 ms</td><td>186 ms</td><td>11.0 GB</td><td>12.5 GB</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">MolmoAct2</th><td>10</td><td>16</td><td>110.4 ms (9 Hz)</td><td>205 ms</td><td>357 ms</td><td>13.0 GB</td><td>15.5 GB</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="note">
            Median GPU model-forward time and peak VRAM for one action-chunk prediction,
            measured in our harness on an NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 (Blackwell) in bfloat16, each
            model at its native default flow/denoising steps. These are model-forward times;
            end-to-end robot-loop latency adds framework-specific CPU preprocessing. Latency
            and memory are architecture-driven and are not a model-quality benchmark. At batch
            size 8, Rho (186 ms) is faster than OpenPI π0.5 (201 ms) and roughly 2× faster than
            MolmoAct2 (357 ms); its 11–12.5 GB footprint fits a single 24 GB GPU.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="efficiency-study">
          <h3>Multiple flow samples improve training efficiency</h3>
          <p>
            We note that using multiple flow samples per observation during
            training can drastically decrease the number of training steps required
            and produce more robust results for a modest increase in VRAM usage and
            training time.
          </p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Condition</th><th>10k</th><th>20k</th><th>30k</th><th>40k</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">GR00T N1.7</th><td>33.50%</td><td>38.63%</td><td>56.38%</td><td>57.63%</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">MolmoAct2, k=1</th><td>49.12%</td><td>57.25%</td><td>62.38%</td><td>59.88%</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">MolmoAct2, k=8</th><td>58.75%</td><td>57.25%</td><td>61.12%</td><td>62.00%</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Rho, k=1</th><td>55.12%</td><td>69.12%</td><td>72.00%</td><td>70.00%</td></tr>
                <tr class="highlight"><th scope="row">Rho, k=8</th><td>67.50%</td><td>67.50%</td><td>72.75%</td><td>74.75%</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">π0.5</th><td>57.75%</td><td>66.38%</td><td>69.88%</td><td>66.50%</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="note">
            RoboEval success rate by training checkpoint. Here, <code>k</code>
            denotes the number of flow samples drawn per observation during training.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

